t}\\)).\n\nHence a *non‑sunny* line must be parallel to one of those families.  \nWe exhibit three distinct non‑sunny lines that together contain every point in (1).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ell_{1}: y=1},\\qquad \n\\boxed{\\ell_{2}: x=1},\\qquad \n\\boxed{\\ell_{3}: x+y=4}.\n\\]\n\nAll three are non‑sunny:\n* \\(\\ell_{1}\\) is horizontal (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis);\n* \\(\\ell_{2}\\) is vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis);\n* \\(\\ell_{3}\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nNow we check each point of (1) individually.\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line(s)? | Verification |\n|----------------|------------------------|---------------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\) | \\(y=1\\) ✓, \\(x=1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(\\ell_{2}\\) | \\(x=1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_{1}\\) | \\(y=1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(\\ell_{2}\\) and \\(\\ell_{3}\\) | \\(x=1\\) ✓, \\(1+3=4\\) ✓ |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{3}\\) | \\(y=1\\) ✓, \\(3+1=4\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(\\ell_{3}\\) | \\(2+2=4\\) ✓ |\n\nEvery required point is on at least one of the three lines, and the lines are distinct.  \nSince each line is parallel to a forbidden direction, the number of sunny lines in this configuration is  \n\n\\[\nk = 0 .\n\\]\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it 