ain at most one point of the smallest diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\)). Hence at most one of the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can pass through \\((1,1)\\). Consequently \\((1,1)\\) must be covered by a sunny line.\n\n   Consider the second smallest diagonal \\(x+y=3\\), which consists of the two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n   No non‑sunny line can contain **both** of them, because the only line through them is \\(x+y=3\\), which is non‑sunny, and the two vertical/horizontal lines through each point are respectively non‑sunny.  \n   Hence at least one of these two points also requires a sunny line.\n\n   Thus at least **two** distinct points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered by sunny lines.  \n   Because a sunny line is never parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=3\\), it can contain at most **one** of the points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n   Therefore we need **at least two** sunny lines to cover the two points of the second diagonal.\n\n   Repeating the argument for the third diagonal \\(x+y=4\\) (which contains three points) shows that at least three distinct sunny lines are necessary to cover that diagonal, because any non‑sunny line can contain at most one of its points and a sunny line can contain at most two of them (otherwise its slope would be \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   Continuing inductively, the diagonal \\(x+y=m\\) (with \\(2\\le m\\le k+1\\)) contains \\(m-1\\) points.  \n   A sunny line can contain at most two of these points (otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\)).  \n   Hence covering the \\(m-1\\) points of the \\(m\\)-th diagonal needs at least \\(\\lceil (m-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   Summing these lower bounds for \\(m=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\) yields a total required number of sunny lines of  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{m=2}^{k+1}\\Big\\lceil\\frac{m-1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n   =\\Big\\lceil\\frac{1}{2}\\Big\\rceil+\\Big\\lceil\\frac{2}{2}\\Big\\rceil+\\cdots+\\Big\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n   >k\\qquad\\text{for }2\\le k\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   (The inequality is strict because the sum equals \\(k\\) only when each term equals \\((m-1)/2\\), which would require all \\((m-1)\\) to be even, i.e. every \\(m\\) odd; this fails for the consecutive integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\).)  \n\n   Hence any configuration that uses fewer than \\(k+1\\) sunny lines cannot cover all points of the first \\(k+1\\) diagonals, and consequently cannot cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).  \n   Therefore no configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for any \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n   The argument is independent of the particular value of \\(n\\) (as long as \\(n\\ge k\\)), so the impossibility holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0,1,n\\) with the impossibility for all intermediate values yields the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) consists of the \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) lattice points inside the right‑isosceles triangle bounded by the axes and the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently the three families of non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\},\n\\]\n\nwith integer constants \\(c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The values \\(k=0,1,n\\) are attainable  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_s : x+y=s,\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1,\n  \\]\n\n  belong to \\(\\mathcal D\\) and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\).  \n  Hence the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\) and none of them is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=3,4,\\dots ,n+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  They cover every point of \\(S_n\\) except \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Add any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  \\sigma : y=x .\n  \\]\n\n  The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and it covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  \\sigma_i :\\; y=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  The slope \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore every \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.  \n  Direct substitution shows  \n\n  \\[\n  \\sigma_i\\cap S_n=\\{(i,1),(1,i)\\},\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. \\(\\sigma_i\\) contains exactly the two points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates are \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\).  \n  As \\(i\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\), the union of the \\(\\sigma_i\\) is precisely \\(S_n\\).  \n  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_n\\) cover all required points.\n\nThus the three values \\(k=0,1,n\\) are realizable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  No configuration exists for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that for some integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSeparate \\(\\mathcal L\\) into  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{sunny}}\\;(k\\text{ lines})\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\;(n-k\\text{ lines}),\n\\]\n\nwhere every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Reduction to the first \\(k+1\\) diagonals  \n\nAmong the non‑sunny families, the diagonal family \\(\\mathcal D\\) is the only one that can contain more than one point of a given diagonal \\(x+y=m\\).  \nConsequently, to maximise the number of points covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) we may (and shall) assume that all its lines belong to \\(\\mathcal D\\); any horizontal or vertical line could be replaced by a diagonal line without decreasing the set of covered points.\n\nChoose the \\(n-k\\) diagonals with the **largest** constants, namely  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n+1,\\;x+y=n,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n-k+2 .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose sum \\(a+b\\) is at least \\(n-k+2\\).  \nThe points that remain uncovered are exactly those on the **first \\(k+1\\) diagonals**\n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nDenote this uncovered set by  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the original covering problem is equivalent to covering the triangle \\(T_k\\) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{sunny}}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  A lower bound on the number of sunny lines needed for \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(m\\) with \\(2\\le m\\le k+1\\) the diagonal \\(x+y=m\\) contains exactly \\(m-1\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  \nA sunny line cannot be parallel to this diagonal; therefore it can contain **at most two** of its points (otherwise the three points would be collinear with slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nHence, to cover the \\(m-1\\) points of the \\(m\\)-th diagonal we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{m-1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n\\]\n\ndifferent sunny lines.\n\nSumming these necessities for all diagonals \\(m=2,\\dots ,k+1\\) gives a global lower bound:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_k &:=\\sum_{m=2}^{k+1}\\Big\\lceil\\frac{m-1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n      =\\sum_{r=1}^{k}\\Big\\lceil\\frac{r}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA straightforward computation shows  \n\n\\[\nN_k=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{k}{2}\\bigl(\\frac{k}{2}+1\\bigr) &\\text{if }k\\text{ is even},\\\\[4pt]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigl(\\frac