to any n-2 columns. We can choose which columns get vertical lines. The solution assumed that the vertical lines are used for columns 1..n-2, but we could choose any n-2 columns. However, we need to cover all points. If we allocate vertical lines to columns 1..n-3 and also to column n-1, that would be n-2 vertical lines covering columns 1..n-3 and n-1. Then columns n-2 and n would be uncovered by vertical lines. But column n-2 has points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). Actually column n-2 has up to 3 points (since b ≤ n+1-(n-2) = 3). So we would need to cover those points with non-vertical lines. That might increase needed lines. So the solution's assumption that the vertical lines must be used for columns 1..n-2 is not necessarily forced; we could choose a different set of columns for vertical lines. However, we need to ensure that we can cover all points with the remaining lines. Let's examine the combinatorial constraints.\n\nWe have n lines total, with k≥2 sunny lines. So at most n-2 non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines are vertical, horizontal, or diagonal. Horizontal and diagonal lines each intersect each column at most one point. So they cannot cover many points per column. So the only way to cover many points in a column is via vertical lines. So we need enough vertical lines to cover the columns that have many points. The columns with many points are the leftmost columns (small a). The column a has n+1-a points. So column 1 has n points, column 2 has n-1 points, etc. So we need vertical lines for columns with many points, else we would need many horizontal/diagonal lines to cover them, which would exceed the line count.\n\nThus, we need to allocate vertical lines to the columns with the largest number of points, i.e., columns 1..(n-2) perhaps. Let's see if we can allocate vertical lines to columns 1..(n-3) and also to column n-1, leaving columns n-2 and n uncovered. Column n-2 has points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). That's three points. Column n has point (n,1). So we