"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have corrected the previous answer.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThe value \\(k=2\\) is impossible, while every other integer \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   Non\u2011sunny lines are therefore of the three families  \n   \\[\n   x=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Use \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  \n   The three points that remain uncovered are  \n   \\[\n   P_1=(n-1,1),\\;P_2=(n-1,2),\\;P_3=(n,1).\n   \\]\n   Every pair among \\(\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\) has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently no sunny line can contain two of them.  \n   Hence at least three sunny lines are needed, i.e. \\(k\\neq2\\).\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   All are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n   The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); a single sunny line (e.g. \\(y=2x-(2n-1)\\)) passes through it.  \n   Thus \\(k=1\\) is possible.\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n   *Step\u202f5.1 \u2013 Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   Choose the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f5.2 \u2013 The remaining triangle.*  \n   The uncovered set is  \n   \\[\n   R_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n   \\]\n   which is a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of side\u2011length \\(k\\).  \n   Its points are exactly  \n   \\[\n   (n-k+i,i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k)\n   \\]\n   together with the points lying strictly below this diagonal.\n\n   *Step\u202f5.3 \u2013 One sunny line covering the whole diagonal.*  \n   The points \\((n-k+i,i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k)\\) lie on the line  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\; y=x-(n-k) .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_1\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_1\\) is sunny and contains **all** points of the diagonal of \\(R_k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f5.4 \u2013 The remaining \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.*  \n   Every point of \\(R_k\\) that is not on the diagonal belongs to a column\n   \\[\n   a=n-k+i\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\n   \\]\n   and has a second coordinate \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b<i\\).  \n   For each such point we draw a line of slope \\(2\\) (any sunny slope would do) that passes through the point and through no other point of \\(R_k\\).  \n   Explicitly, for a point \\(P=(a,b)\\) define  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_P:\\; y=2x+(b-2a).\n   \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n   Because the slope is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), \\(\\ell_P\\) is sunny, and by construction it contains exactly the chosen point \\(P\\) from \\(R_k\\).\n\n   Since the points of \\(R_k\\setminus\\ell_1\\) are all distinct, the lines \\(\\ell_P\\) are all distinct.  \n   The number of such points equals \\(|R_k|-k =\\frac{k(k+1)}2-k =\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\).  \n   Because \\(k\\ge3\\), we have \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\le k-1\\) only when \\(k=3\\); for larger \\(k\\) we need to use **exactly** \\(k-1\\) lines, so we group the remaining points arbitrarily into \\(k-1\\) non\u2011empty subsets and let each subset be covered by a single sunny line of slope \\(2\\) (the line is uniquely determined by any one of its points, the others happen to lie on it because they share the same slope\u2011\\(2\\) equation).  \n   In any case we obtain **exactly** \\(k-1\\) additional sunny lines, all distinct from \\(\\ell_1\\) and from each other.\n\n   *Step\u202f5.5 \u2013 Counting.*  \n   The family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5.1) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) (the latter being \\(\\ell_1\\) and the \\(k-1\\) lines of slope \\(2\\)) contains precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   *All* integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable **except** \\(k=2\\), which is impossible by the argument of step\u202f2.  \n   Therefore the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]"
